<section aria-labelledby="usage-checks"><h2 id="usage-checks">Usage checks · 用法辨析</h2>
<p>A few distinctions to keep clear when reviewing the original notes.</p>
<div class="reading-checks">
<article><h3>waive ≠ wave</h3><p><strong>Waive a fee</strong> means choose not to charge it. <strong>Wave</strong> refers to a hand movement or a moving swell of water.</p><p class="reading-translation" lang="zh-Hant"><strong>waive</strong> 是「免除」，例如免收費用；<strong>wave</strong> 則是「揮手」或「波浪」。注意拼字和意思的差別。</p><p class="reading-example">The hotel waived the cancellation fee.<span class="reading-translation" lang="zh-Hant">飯店免收了取消費用。</span></p></article>
<article><h3>due · dues · due by</h3><p><strong>Due</strong> is commonly an adjective: a payment is owed or expected. <strong>Dues</strong> are membership fees. <strong>Due by Friday</strong> gives the latest deadline.</p><p class="reading-translation" lang="zh-Hant"><strong>due</strong> 常作形容詞，表示「到期應付的」；<strong>dues</strong> 是「會費」。<strong>due by Friday</strong> 表示最晚須在週五繳交或完成，by 含「不晚於」之意。</p><p class="reading-example">The balance is due by Friday. Membership dues are payable annually.<span class="reading-translation" lang="zh-Hant">餘款最晚須於週五繳清。會員會費按年繳納。</span></p></article>
<article><h3>competent · compulsory</h3><p>Both are <strong>adjectives</strong>. Competent describes ability; compulsory describes a requirement.</p><p class="reading-translation" lang="zh-Hant">兩者都是<strong>形容詞</strong>：<strong>competent</strong> 指「能勝任的」，<strong>compulsory</strong> 指「強制性的、必須參加的」。</p><p class="reading-example">A competent instructor leads the compulsory training.<span class="reading-translation" lang="zh-Hant">由一位能勝任的講師帶領這項強制訓練。</span></p></article>
<article><h3>virtually</h3><p>Do not automatically read this as “online.” It often means <strong>almost</strong>. The surrounding words decide the sense.</p><p class="reading-translation" lang="zh-Hant">常見意思是「<strong>幾乎</strong>」，不要一看到 virtually 就翻成「虛擬地」。例如 virtually identical 是「幾乎相同」；meet virtually 才是「以線上方式開會」。</p><p class="reading-example">The two proposals are virtually identical.<span class="reading-translation" lang="zh-Hant">這兩份提案幾乎完全相同。</span></p></article>
<article><h3>reference ≠ letter</h3><p>A <strong>personal reference</strong> may be a person who recommends you or a recommendation about your character. A <strong>reference letter</strong> specifically names the document.</p><p class="reading-translation" lang="zh-Hant"><strong>personal reference</strong> 可能指「品格推薦人」或「對品格的推薦」，不一定是一封信；<strong>reference letter</strong> 才明確指「推薦信」這份文件。</p><p class="reading-example">Please provide two references and one reference letter.<span class="reading-translation" lang="zh-Hant">請提供兩位推薦人的資料及一封推薦信。</span></p></article>
<article><h3>resident ≠ occupant in every context</h3><p>A resident lives in a place. An occupant occupies a space, building, or vehicle and need not live there.</p><p class="reading-translation" lang="zh-Hant"><strong>resident</strong> 是住在某地的「居民、住戶」；<strong>occupant</strong> 是某空間的「使用者、占用者」，也可能是在辦公室工作或車內的人，兩者不能處處互換。</p><p class="reading-example">Office occupants should use the rear exit.<span class="reading-translation" lang="zh-Hant">辦公室內的人員應使用後方出口。</span></p></article>
</div></section>

<section aria-labelledby="sentence-patterns"><h2 id="sentence-patterns">Sentence patterns · 句型整理</h2>
<div class="reading-table-wrap" role="region" aria-label="Sentence patterns" tabindex="0"><table class="reading-table"><caption>Reusable structures from the Test 1 review</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Pattern</th><th scope="col">Use</th><th scope="col">Example</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><th scope="row">be eligible to + verb / for + noun</th><td>Qualification<span class="reading-translation" lang="zh-Hant">表示符合資格；to 後接原形動詞，for 後接名詞。</span></td><td>Members are eligible to apply for a grant.<span class="reading-translation" lang="zh-Hant">會員符合申請補助金的資格。</span></td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">be due by + deadline</th><td>The latest allowed time<span class="reading-translation" lang="zh-Hant">表示最晚期限；by 指「不晚於」。</span></td><td>The report is due by noon.<span class="reading-translation" lang="zh-Hant">報告最晚須於中午前繳交。</span></td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">as though + clause</th><td>An apparent or imagined situation<span class="reading-translation" lang="zh-Hant">彷彿、好像；後接完整子句。</span></td><td>She spoke as though she knew the answer.<span class="reading-translation" lang="zh-Hant">她說話的樣子彷彿知道答案。</span></td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">of all + plural noun</th><td>A whole comparison group<span class="reading-translation" lang="zh-Hant">在所有……之中；用來帶出比較範圍。</span></td><td>Of all the proposals, this one is the clearest.<span class="reading-translation" lang="zh-Hant">在所有提案中，這一份最清楚。</span></td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">as for + noun phrase</th><td>A shift to a particular topic<span class="reading-translation" lang="zh-Hant">至於……；轉而談某個主題。</span></td><td>As for the schedule, we can discuss it tomorrow.<span class="reading-translation" lang="zh-Hant">至於時程，我們明天可以再討論。</span></td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">on behalf of + person / group</th><td>Representation<span class="reading-translation" lang="zh-Hant">代表某人或團體。</span></td><td>I am writing on behalf of the committee.<span class="reading-translation" lang="zh-Hant">我代表委員會寫這封信。</span></td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">rest assured that + clause</th><td>Reassurance<span class="reading-translation" lang="zh-Hant">請放心……；that 後接讓對方安心的內容。</span></td><td>Rest assured that your booking is confirmed.<span class="reading-translation" lang="zh-Hant">請放心，您的預訂已經確認。</span></td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">specialize in + noun / -ing</th><td>A field of expertise<span class="reading-translation" lang="zh-Hant">專精於……；in 後接名詞或動名詞。</span></td><td>The firm specializes in resolving contract disputes.<span class="reading-translation" lang="zh-Hant">這間事務所專門處理合約糾紛。</span></td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">rely on / upon + noun</th><td>Dependence<span class="reading-translation" lang="zh-Hant">依賴、仰賴……；on 與 upon 都可使用。</span></td><td>We rely upon local volunteers.<span class="reading-translation" lang="zh-Hant">我們仰賴當地志工的協助。</span></td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">be accompanied by + noun</th><td>Something or someone that comes with another<span class="reading-translation" lang="zh-Hant">伴隨著……；申請文件也可用此句型表示須檢附附件。</span></td><td>Each application must be accompanied by a résumé.<span class="reading-translation" lang="zh-Hant">每份申請都必須附上履歷。</span></td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">push for + noun</th><td>Support a change or try to obtain it<span class="reading-translation" lang="zh-Hant">積極爭取、推動某事。</span></td><td>Residents are pushing for safer crossings.<span class="reading-translation" lang="zh-Hant">居民正在爭取更安全的行人穿越設施。</span></td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">take place</th><td>An event happens<span class="reading-translation" lang="zh-Hant">舉行、發生；用於描述活動，不用被動語態。</span></td><td>The ceremony will take place in the main hall.<span class="reading-translation" lang="zh-Hant">典禮將在大廳舉行。</span></td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">appreciate / anticipate + -ing</th><td>Follow these verbs with a noun or gerund<span class="reading-translation" lang="zh-Hant">後接名詞或動名詞：appreciate doing、anticipate doing。</span></td><td>We appreciate being informed and anticipate receiving an update.<span class="reading-translation" lang="zh-Hant">我們感謝對方告知情況，並預期會收到進一步消息。</span></td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
</section>

